---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const REPO = "https://github.com/rainmanjam/polyemesis";
---

<Base
  title="Install polyemesis — Docker, installer or static binary"
  description="Run polyemesis with Docker, a one-line installer, or a single static binary. No account, nothing phoning home, no hosted tier. MIT licensed."
  path="/download"
>
  <section class="wrap py-16 sm:py-20">
    <p class="eyebrow">Install</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-balance text-4xl font-bold leading-tight sm:text-5xl">
      One binary, or one container.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-pretty text-lg leading-relaxed text-muted">
      No account to create, nothing phoning home, no hosted tier to graduate
      to. It can publish its own state over MQTT, but that is off by default and
      goes to a broker you name. The
      only runtime dependency is FFmpeg, and the container bundles it.
      Transcription is the one optional extra that wants a second binary,
      whisper.cpp.
    </p>
    {/* THE TWO DOCUMENTS THE README SENDS PEOPLE TO, at the top of the page
        rather than buried under four install cards. Choosing an install method
        is the smallest decision on this page; whether you can operate the thing
        afterwards is the real one, and these are what answer it. Both render
        from docs/ at build time, so neither can drift from the software. */}
    {/* NOT ACCENT-COLOURED AT REST, matching index.astro's hero. The accent
        budget docs/DESIGN-SYSTEM.md sets is the home page's announcement bar
        and the primary CTA; a standing arrow link in the same hue as
        the one filled button on the page competes with it for the same
        meaning. A neutral resting underline carries the affordance (no arrow:
        these are a pair of sibling links, and arrows mark a primary link
        only), and the accent arrives on approach.
        Inline links inside prose are deliberately NOT changed — see
        `.doc-prose a` in global.css. There, colour separates a link from the
        sentence around it and removing it would cost a reader something real;
        here it decorates a link that is already unmistakably a link. */}
    <p class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 text-sm">
      <a href="/docs/quickstart" class="tap text-fg underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-primary-bright hover:decoration-primary-bright"
        >Quickstart: from install to a first stream</a
      >
      <a href="/docs/install" class="tap text-fg underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-primary-bright hover:decoration-primary-bright"
        >Install guide: per-platform, service files, GPU images</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* md: as well as lg:, because the tablet band had nothing.

      `md:` appears four times in this whole site and all four are the Nav
      showing and hiding -- not one is a grid. So every layout here went from
      one column straight to lg:, and 768px got the PHONE layout at desktop
      type. Measured: this page is 3122px at 768 against 2294px at 1440, 36%
      taller for the same content, with half the width unused.

      Two install cards side by side at 768 gives each ~360px, which the
      commands fit now that they wrap. */}
  <section class="wrap pb-16">
    <div class="card-grid card-grid--2">
      <div class="card p-6">
        <div class="flex items-baseline justify-between gap-3">
          <h2 class="text-lg font-medium">Docker</h2>
          <span class="chip">recommended</span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          FFmpeg is pinned and bundled, so the SRT support is known-good. This is
          the build the container test suites run against.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="docker"
          code={`docker run -d --name polyemesis \\
    -p 8080:8080 -p 6000:6000/udp -p 1935:1935 \\
    -v polyemesis-data:/data \\
    rainmanjam/polyemesis:latest`}
        />
        {/* A DEFINITION LIST, NOT A DOT-SEPARATED LINE (2026-11 design review).
            Three ports are three facts a reader looks up one at a time, usually
            while writing a firewall rule, so each gets its own row. */}
        <dl class="mt-3 grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-[12px]">
          <dt class="text-subtle">UI</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-fg">:8080</dd>
          <dt class="text-subtle">SRT ingest</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-fg">:6000/udp</dd>
          <dt class="text-subtle">RTMP ingest</dt>
          <dd class="font-mono text-fg">:1935</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          Note the <span class="font-mono">/udp</span> on port 6000. SRT is UDP,
          and omitting the suffix is the classic reason an ingest silently
          receives nothing. 1935 is published up front because a container's port
          mappings are fixed when it is created: an RTMP source added later has
          nothing listening for it, and says so nowhere.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium">Installer script</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Asks what you want, then sets up Docker or a static binary with a
          systemd unit, a data directory and TLS. Read it before you pipe it to a
          root shell. It is long, but it is one file and declares every flag up
          front.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="install.sh"
          code={`curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/\\
  rainmanjam/polyemesis/main/scripts/install.sh \\
    | sudo bash -s -- --tls selfsigned`}
        />
        <p class="mt-3 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          It installs a self-signed certificate unless told otherwise. For Let's
          Encrypt pass <span class="font-mono">--tls acme</span> with{" "}
          <span class="font-mono">--hostname</span> and{" "}
          <span class="font-mono">--email</span>; it refuses acme without both.
          Behind a reverse proxy that already terminates TLS, pass{" "}
          <span class="font-mono">--tls off</span>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          In binary mode it also installs the fonts burned-in captions are drawn
          with (fontconfig, DejaVu and Noto CJK), so Chinese, Japanese, Korean,
          Arabic and Hebrew captions render out of the box. CJK is the large
          part (60–190 MB by distribution);{" "}
          <span class="font-mono">--no-cjk-fonts</span> leaves it out.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium">Static binary</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The UI is embedded. Pure-Go SQLite, no cgo. You supply FFmpeg, and it
          needs one built with <span class="font-mono">--enable-libsrt</span> if
          you want multitrack ingest.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a href={`${REPO}/releases/latest`} rel="noopener" class="btn btn-ghost text-sm">
            Releases on GitHub →
          </a>
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          Homebrew's FFmpeg on macOS ships without SRT. polyemesis needs FFmpeg
          8.0 or later, which carries SRT and Enhanced RTMP multitrack alike;
          Ubuntu 26.04 packages it, the installer can fetch a pinned 8.1 build,
          and the container bundles one. (OBS does not send multitrack audio over
          RTMP at any version we have tested, so for OBS users SRT is the path.)
        </p>
        <p class="mt-2 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          Linux is the operated target, and the broadcast path is tested on
          Windows and macOS in CI too; that suite has caught Windows-only bugs
          before they shipped. What is not yet proven on Windows is long-running
          service operation: the service wrapper is unexercised and recording can
          truncate on service stop. Run it on Linux for production, and on Windows
          or macOS to build and evaluate.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* BUILD FROM A CLONE, which the site did not offer at all — the static
          binary card sent a reader to the releases page and stopped. This is a
          single static Go binary with `make build` as its own README's second
          install path, and the audience that self-hosts a broadcast server is the
          audience most likely to want to read the source it is running first.

          The block is README.md's "Try it" verbatim, comment included. A build
          recipe that is nearly right is worse than none: `make build` is what
          embeds the UI, and a reader who runs `go build` instead gets a binary
          that starts and serves nothing. */}
      <div class="card p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium">Build from a clone</h2>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Go 1.27.0+, Node 20.19+/22.12+ and FFmpeg. The UI is built and embedded
          by the same target, so there is one artefact at the end of it.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock
          label="make"
          code={`git clone https://github.com/rainmanjam/polyemesis
  cd polyemesis
  make build            # builds the UI, embeds it, produces ./polyemesis
  ./polyemesis -data ./data`}
        />
        <p class="mt-3 text-[11px] leading-relaxed text-subtle">
          <span class="font-mono">make check</span> runs go vet, the Go test suite
          and the UI typecheck; <span class="font-mono">make help</span> lists
          every target. The contributing guide states the constraints that are not
          negotiable, so you find out before writing rather than after.
        </p>
      </div>

      {/* SPANS BOTH COLUMNS, and not only to avoid the orphan a fifth card would
          leave on the last row. The four above are alternatives — pick one — and
          this one is what everybody does next whichever they picked, so the card
          that reads differently is the one that IS different. */}
      <div class="card card-span-full p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-medium">Then point OBS at it</h2>
        <ol class="mt-3 space-y-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <li>
            <span class="font-mono text-subtle">1.</span> Open the UI and set an admin
            password. If it is ever lost, <span class="font-mono">-reset-admin</span> on
            the box sets a new one; there is no recovery from anywhere else.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-mono text-subtle">2.</span> Choose an ingest mode. A fresh
            install has none: SRT carries many tracks from many sources and is what the
            routing needs; RTMP shares its port between sources the same way, told apart
            by the stream key, and carries one audio track unless the publisher sends Enhanced RTMP. Then
            copy the ingest URL: it carries the credential that addresses this source.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-mono text-subtle">3.</span> In OBS, Settings → Output →
            Advanced → Recording, set Type to “Custom Output (FFmpeg)”, FFmpeg Output
            Type to “Output to URL”, container <span class="font-mono">mpegts</span>,
            and tick the audio tracks to send. Paste the ingest URL, then press Start{" "}
            <em>Recording</em>. With Output to URL, that is the SRT push.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="font-mono text-subtle">4.</span> Add destinations, then tick
            which tracks each one receives.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="mt-4">
          <a
            href="/how-to-multistream-from-obs"
            class="inline-block py-1 text-sm text-fg transition-colors hover:text-primary-bright hover:underline"
          >
            The full OBS guide →
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/* HowTo, describing the four steps rendered immediately above and nothing
        else. Every step text is a condensation of the visible <li> it names — if
        the list changes and this does not, the markup describes a page that no
        longer exists, which is worse than having no structured data at all.

        The one Google-eligible schema this site actually earns: these are
        sequential setup instructions with a real ordered list behind them, which
        is the whole test. FAQPage was considered for the "Is this for you?"
        block on the home page and rejected — that is two bullet lists, not
        question-and-answer pairs, and marking it up as FAQ would be a claim
        about content that is not there.

        Outside the card grid, not in it: a <script> child still counts in the
        grid's :nth-child() positions, which .card-grid--5 places cards by. */}
    <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "HowTo",
      name: "Send OBS to polyemesis over multitrack SRT",
      description:
        "Point OBS at a self-hosted polyemesis server so each destination can " +
        "receive its own audio mix, built from the split tracks OBS sends.",
      totalTime: "PT10M",
      tool: [{ "@type": "HowToTool", name: "OBS Studio" }],
      supply: [{ "@type": "HowToSupply", name: "A running polyemesis server" }],
      step: [
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          name: "Set an admin password",
          text:
            "Open the UI and set an admin password. If it is lost, -reset-admin " +
            "on the box sets a new one; there is no recovery from anywhere else.",
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          name: "Choose an ingest mode",
          text:
            "A fresh install has none. SRT carries many tracks from many sources " +
            "and is what the routing needs. RTMP shares its port between sources, " +
            "told apart by the stream key, and carries one audio track unless the " +
            "publisher sends Enhanced RTMP. Copy the ingest URL: it carries the credential " +
            "that addresses this source.",
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          name: "Configure OBS for multitrack SRT",
          text:
            "In OBS, Settings, Output, Advanced, Recording: set Type to Custom " +
            "Output (FFmpeg), FFmpeg Output Type to Output to URL, container " +
            "mpegts, and tick the audio tracks to send. Paste the ingest URL, " +
            "then press Start Recording. With Output to URL, that is the SRT push.",
          url: "https://polyemesis.com/download",
        },
        {
          "@type": "HowToStep",
          name: "Add destinations and route the audio",
          text: "Add destinations, then tick which tracks each one receives.",
        },
      ],
    })} />
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-line bg-surface/30 py-16">
    <div class="wrap max-w-3xl">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-medium">What it needs from the machine</h2>
      <dl class="mt-6 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
        {
          [
            ["FFmpeg", "The only dependency the server itself needs. Needs libsrt for SRT ingest. Without it you fall back to RTMP, which carries one audio track on classic RTMP. FFmpeg 8.0 or later is required; 8.x is what the container ships, and CI tests the latest 8.x and 9.x."],
            ["CPU", "Video is copied, not re-encoded, so cost is nearly independent of resolution. Only audio is decoded, mixed and re-encoded to AAC. Renditions are the exception: those are a real encode, and the UI estimates what one will cost before you save it."],
            ["Ports", "One TCP port for the UI and API. One UDP port for SRT, shared by every source. RTMP is optional and works the same way: one TCP port, shared by every source that uses it."],
            ["Disk", "Only if you record. Retention is bounded by age, total size, and a free-space floor that accounts for files polyemesis did not write."],
          ].map(([t, d]) => (
            <div class="py-4">
              <dt class="text-[15px] font-medium text-fg">{t}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">{d}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
